 Younger, retrospectively influenced the perception and historical context of Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus's legacy."}, {"sourceSlug": "tiberius-sempronius-gracchus", "sourceName": "Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "roman-republic", "targetName": "Roman Republic", "context": "Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus held two consulships and received two triumphs, actively participating in the political and military governance of the Roman Republic."}, {"sourceSlug": "tiberius-sempronius-gracchus", "sourceName": "Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "sardinia", "targetName": "Sardinia", "context": "Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus conducted successful military campaigns against rebellious forces in Sardinia during his first consulship in 177 BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Political Center"}, {"name": "Sardinia, Italy", "role": "Military Campaign Site"}, {"name": "Zama, Tunisia", "role": "Battle Participation"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "tiberius-sempronius-gracchus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q443505", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiberius_Sempronius_Gracchus_(consul_177_BC)"], "tags": ["Political Leaders", "political-systems", "geopolitical-linkage", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus was a pivotal figure in the post-Second Punic War Roman Republic, contributing significantly to its military consolidation and establishing a powerful lineage that shaped later Roman political reforms through his sons.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-24T07:02:30Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#30073893614", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus was a Roman politician and general of the 2nd century BC. He served two consulships, one in 177 and one 163 BC, and was awarded two triumphs. He was also the father of the two famous Gracchi brothers: Tiberius and Gaius.", "newValue": "Tiberius Sempronius Gracchus (c. 219–200 BCE) was a distinguished Roman politician and general whose career unfolded during the tumultuous Second Punic War and its immediate aftermath, a period that fundamentally reshaped the Roman Republic's military and political landscape. His significant contributions to Rome's victory over Carthage, particularly his command in Spain and his diplomatic efforts, cemented his reputation as a capable leader and statesman, laying a foundational legacy for his more famous sons, the Gracchi brothers.

Gracchus first achieved prominence during the Second Punic War, serving as a military tribune under Scipio Africanus during the pivotal Battle of Zama in 202 BCE, which decisively ended Carthaginian resistance in Africa. Following the war, he held two consulships, first in 177 BCE and again in 163 BCE. His first consulship saw him campaign successfully against the Sardinians, leading to a triumph in 175 BCE. During his second consulship, he again distinguished himself, particularly in diplomatic missions and administrative roles, further solidifying Roman control in various provinces. He was awarded a second triumph for his military successes.

